P=[
("Alexander Graham Bell",1847,"1847–1922","Inventor and teacher of the deaf","Scotland, USA and Canada","The telephone","Born in Edinburgh to a family of speech teachers — his mother and wife were deaf — Bell emigrated to Canada and then Boston, where he taught deaf students. Experimenting with transmitting multiple telegraph messages, he and Thomas Watson developed the telephone, patented on 7 March 1876, hours after his lawyer beat Elisha Gray's filing; the first call followed on 10 March. The Bell Telephone Company grew into AT&T. Bell later worked on the photophone, hydrofoils and aircraft, and co-founded the National Geographic Society. His support for eugenic ideas about deaf people and for suppressing sign language is now criticised.","Alexander_Graham_Bell","S","First telephone call; Telephone; Antonio Meucci; United States"),
("Antonio Meucci",1808,"1808–1889","Inventor","Italy and USA","Early voice-transmission device","Meucci, a Florentine stage technician, emigrated to Cuba and then Staten Island, New York, where he developed a 'telettrofono' to talk between his workshop and his paralysed wife's bedroom. Poor and speaking little English, he filed a patent caveat in 1871 but could not afford to renew it after 1874. When Bell patented the telephone, Meucci sued; the case ended with his death. In 2002 the US House of Representatives recognised his contribution.","Antonio_Meucci","S","Telephone; Alexander Graham Bell; First telephone call; United States"),
("Wright brothers",1867,"Wilbur 1867–1912; Orville 1871–1948","Aviation pioneers","Dayton, Ohio, USA","First controlled powered aeroplane","Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop and never finished high school. Inspired by glider pioneer Otto Lilienthal's death, they studied flight control, built a wind tunnel and invented wing-warping. On 17 December 1903 at Kitty Hawk they made four flights, the longest 260 m. They spent years in patent battles with Glenn Curtiss, which slowed American aviation; Wilbur died of typhoid in 1912.","Wright_brothers","S","Aeroplane; Wright brothers' flight; World War I; United States"),
("Guglielmo Marconi",1874,"1874–1937","Inventor","Italy and Britain","Radio telegraphy","Marconi, from a wealthy family in Bologna, built on Hertz's work to develop long-distance wireless telegraphy, moving to Britain in 1896 to patent it. He claimed to receive the first transatlantic signal in 1901 and shared the 1909 Nobel Prize. His company's operators on the Titanic sent distress calls in 1912. In 1943 the US Supreme Court upheld earlier patents of Tesla and others. He later joined Mussolini's Fascist party.","Guglielmo_Marconi","S","Radio; Marconi's transatlantic radio signal; Jagadish Chandra Bose; Nikola Tesla; Benito Mussolini"),
("J. Robert Oppenheimer",1904,"1904–1967","Physicist","USA","Director of Los Alamos; 'father of the atomic bomb'","Oppenheimer, a brilliant theoretical physicist who read the Bhagavad Gita in Sanskrit, led the Los Alamos laboratory of the Manhattan Project from 1943. After the Trinity test he recalled the line 'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds'. After the war he chaired the Atomic Energy Commission's advisory committee and opposed the hydrogen bomb. In 1954, amid McCarthyism, a hearing revoked his security clearance over past communist associations; the US Department of Energy vacated that decision in 2022.","J._Robert_Oppenheimer","S","Atomic bomb; Manhattan Project begins; Trinity nuclear test; Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki; World War II; Albert Einstein; Cold War"),
("Enrico Fermi",1901,"1901–1954","Physicist","Italy and USA","First nuclear reactor","Fermi won the 1938 Nobel Prize for work on neutron-induced radioactivity and used the trip to Stockholm to flee Fascist Italy, as his wife was Jewish. In Chicago he led the team that built Chicago Pile-1, achieving the first controlled nuclear chain reaction on 2 December 1942, and worked on the Manhattan Project.","Enrico_Fermi","S","Nuclear energy; Atomic bomb; Manhattan Project begins; Benito Mussolini"),
("Lise Meitner",1878,"1878–1968","Physicist","Austria, Germany, Sweden","Explained nuclear fission","Meitner, one of the first women to earn a physics doctorate in Vienna, worked with Otto Hahn in Berlin for 30 years. As a Jew she fled Nazi Germany in 1938. With her nephew Otto Frisch she explained Hahn's results as nuclear fission. Hahn alone received the 1944 Nobel Prize. She refused to work on the atomic bomb. Element 109, meitnerium, honours her.","Lise_Meitner","S","Nuclear energy; Atomic bomb; Adolf Hitler; Judaism"),
("Alexander Fleming",1881,"1881–1955","Bacteriologist","Scotland and London","Discovery of penicillin","Fleming served as a doctor in WWI, seeing soldiers die of infected wounds. In 1928 at St Mary's Hospital he noticed Penicillium mould killing bacteria. He shared the 1945 Nobel Prize with Florey and Chain, who turned it into a drug, and warned about antibiotic resistance.","Alexander_Fleming","S","Antibiotics; Discovery of penicillin; World War I; World War II"),
("Johannes Gutenberg",1400,"c. 1400–1468","Inventor and printer","Mainz, Germany","Movable-type printing press","Gutenberg, a goldsmith, developed the European movable-type printing system around 1440–1450 and printed the Gutenberg Bible. He lost his workshop to his financier Johann Fust in a lawsuit in 1455 and died with little recognition.","Johannes_Gutenberg","S","Printing press; Gutenberg printing press; Gutenberg Bible; Protestant Reformation; Renaissance"),
("James Watt",1736,"1736–1819","Engineer","Scotland","Improved steam engine","Watt, an instrument maker at Glasgow University, added a separate condenser to the steam engine (1765) and, with Matthew Boulton, built rotary engines that powered factories. The unit of power, the watt, honours him.","James_Watt","S","Steam engine; Watt's steam engine; Industrial Revolution"),
("Edward Jenner",1749,"1749–1823","Doctor","England","Smallpox vaccine","Jenner tested cowpox inoculation on James Phipps in 1796 and promoted vaccination worldwide, sending vaccine to India by 1802. Smallpox was eradicated in 1980.","Edward_Jenner","S","Vaccination; Smallpox in the Americas"),
("Tim Berners-Lee",1955,"born 1955","Computer scientist","Britain and CERN","World Wide Web","Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web at CERN in 1989, wrote HTML, HTTP and the first browser, and gave it to the world royalty-free. He founded the W3C and campaigns for an open web and data privacy.","Tim_Berners-Lee","S","Internet; Invention of the World Wide Web; ARPANET's first message"),
("Rosalind Franklin",1920,"1920–1958","Chemist","Britain","X-ray images of DNA","Franklin's X-ray diffraction work at King's College London, especially Photo 51, was key to the double-helix model, shown to Watson without her knowledge. She also studied viruses and coal. She died of ovarian cancer at 37, four years before the Nobel Prize she could not share.","Rosalind_Franklin","S","DNA double helix; Discovery of the DNA double helix"),
("Wernher von Braun",1912,"1912–1977","Rocket engineer","Germany and USA","V-2 and Saturn V","Von Braun joined the Nazi Party and SS and led development of the V-2 missile, built by concentration camp prisoners. Brought to the US under Operation Paperclip, he led NASA's Saturn V rocket that took Apollo astronauts to the Moon.","Wernher_von_Braun","T","Space rocket; Operation Paperclip; World War II; Moon landing; Adolf Hitler"),
("Henry Ford",1863,"1863–1947","Industrialist","USA","Mass-produced cars","Ford's moving assembly line (1913) made the Model T affordable and doubled workers' wages to $5 a day. He also published antisemitic propaganda, including the forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion, praised by Hitler.","Henry_Ford","T","Automobile; Protocols of the Elders of Zion; Adolf Hitler; United States"),
("Steve Jobs",1955,"1955–2011","Entrepreneur","USA","Apple, the iPhone","Jobs co-founded Apple in 1976, was ousted in 1985, returned in 1997, and launched the iPod, iPhone and iPad. He visited India in 1974 seeking spiritual guidance.","Steve_Jobs","S","Smartphone; Launch of the iPhone; United States"),
]
